d them when i was coming back  from the capitol just people  milling about in the halls um and  dressed in apparel that they were  presumably in town for uh the the  event that they ultimately attended uh  and so it was  notable and and certainly concerning uh  you know initially from a pandemic  perspective but  um ultimately because of the  insurrectionist attack  yeah i mean it's it's terrifying the  more we see the more we hear i'm sorry  that you had to go through that your  democratic colleague  mikey cheryl says she believes that some  of these people had  help from some of your fellow colleagues  some of your fellow congress people  do you believe that's the case well so  that would be the only way that these  individuals would have entered the  building  um i have uh been told that they entered  with members of congress  you know notably some of the challenges  that exist is that members of congress  have a lot more liberties when it comes  to walking around the capitol when it  comes you know and understandably so  uh but even during a shutdown period  because of  the pandemic it was limited to staff but  members of congress  are able to bring people in uh we do  have the  uh i guess the they give us the courtesy  of whoever we deem can come in despite  the closures  uh they they allow it um or certainly  don't argue back  so the way that these individuals would  have entered the capital in the first  place would have been with a member of  congress  making that possible now the letter has  been signed by more than 30 democrats  but so far  no republicans have signed the letter to  my knowledge have any of them  expressed any of their concerns about  that to you either  privately uh so  in the larger discussion of all that's  been happening with the  the domestic violent extremists who were  uh who waged an attack on our capital um  you know i don't know that  they are or not thinking about it  privately but um  it hasn't risen up to the level of a  discus